## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its security frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto). .NET on macOS relied on those native libraries, so starting in **.NET 11**, `DSA.Create()` throws a **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. Linux (OpenSSL) and Windows (CNG) still support it, which is why only macOS breaks.

This was telegraphed by deprecation warnings in earlier .NET versions (the `SYSLIB0XXX` obsoletion on DSA APIs).

## How to fix it

You have two options:

### Option 1 – Migrate away from DSA (preferred)
If you control the signing side, switch to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**. DSA is a legacy algorithm and no modern framework recommends it.

### Option 2 – Use a managed/cross-platform DSA implementation
If you **must** verify existing legacy DSA signatures, use a library that doesn't depend on the OS crypto stack:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

Then replace the platform-dependent call:

```csharp
// Before (.NET 10, breaks on macOS in .NET 11)
using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyParams);
bool ok = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);

// After (cross-platform via BouncyCastle)
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var pubKey = DotNetUtilities.GetDsaPublicKey(legacyParams);
var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
verifier.Init(forSigning: false, pubKey);
verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool ok = verifier.VerifySigned(signature);
```

This works identically on Linux, Windows, and macOS regardless of OS crypto support.
